WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.440
Versailles is often described as&nbsp;
the greatest palace in Europe.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.240
But its real achievement is&nbsp;
not its size or its luxury.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.640
What makes Versailles extraordinary&nbsp;
is that it was conceived as a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.440
architectural composition,&nbsp;
where the palace, the gardens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:31.840
and even the surrounding town are all organized&nbsp;
around one central idea: the king himself.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.240
To understand why Versailles looks the way it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.200
does, we have to begin with a&nbsp;
surprisingly modest building.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.960
In the 1630s King Louis XIII built&nbsp;
a small château at Versailles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.080
a small town southwest of Paris&nbsp;
surrounded by large forest areas.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.280
The building was a modest brick structure&nbsp;
with details in stone and a slate roof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.440
a typical composition of the French&nbsp;
Renaissance architecture of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.480
as seen in the Place des Vosges in Paris.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.160
The plan was organized around&nbsp;
a rectangular courtyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.160
and flanked by two projecting wings opening&nbsp;
the space to the east towards Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:19.520
while a low arcade partially enclosed&nbsp;
the space to form an entrance courtyard.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.080
The château was surrounded by a moat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.000
and small gardens were laid out&nbsp;
in the immediate surroundings.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.280
Louis XIII frequently stayed at Versailles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.280
and used this building to rest&nbsp;
after hunting in the nearby forests.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:42.000
After Louis XIII’s death in 1643, the palace&nbsp;
remained pretty much abandoned for nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.320
two decades, until 1661, when his son, Louis&nbsp;
XIV, commenced his personal rule and showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:57.600
interest in Versailles. Louis XIV understood that&nbsp;
architecture could become a political instrument.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.560
He wanted to centralize the power in the&nbsp;
monarchy by drawing the French nobility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:09.600
away from their regional strongholds into a&nbsp;
royal court that revolved entirely around him.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.280
He intended to move the royal court out of&nbsp;
Paris to Versailles, as he dismissed medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.200
Paris because of its political instability, while&nbsp;
Versailles offered a setting where he could keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.520
the court under his direct supervision&nbsp;
and reinforce his absolute authority.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.880
Instead of simply enlarging his father’s&nbsp;
château, he set out to transform Versailles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.840
into a fit setting worthy of the&nbsp;
Sun King, as he called himself.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.480
To accomplish this, he commissioned&nbsp;
the landscape architect André Le Notre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:48.080
to transform the grounds into one of the&nbsp;
most ambitious system of gardens ever built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.720
where architecture could&nbsp;
become a symbol of his power.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.600
André Le Notre designed an immense&nbsp;
composition of terraces, sculptures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.680
fountains, water basins, and tree-lined avenues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:10.800
integrated into a grand plan organized around&nbsp;
the king’s bedroom at the center of the palace.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.400
At Versailles, the true protagonist&nbsp;
is the great longitudinal axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.160
every other element is related&nbsp;
and subordinated to it.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.760
This axis extends from the&nbsp;
palace through the gardens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.440
but also in the opposite&nbsp;
direction toward the town.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.680
The palace therefore becomes an&nbsp;
edge that divides the axis into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.960
two different conditions, the gardens&nbsp;
on one side, and the town on the other.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:45.600
The gardens are structured around two principal&nbsp;
axes: a primary axis running east-west and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.840
secondary axis running north-south, both&nbsp;
articulated by great expanses of water.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.840
Along the main axis are placed the two most&nbsp;
beautiful fountains: the basin of Latona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.680
with its magnificent gilded statues, depicts&nbsp;
the Greek goddess protecting her children as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:10.320
peasants who denied her water are transformed into&nbsp;
frogs, turtles, and lizards, from whose mouths the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:19.200
water now flows, and the basin of Apollo, god of&nbsp;
the sun, an unmistakable reference to the king.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.200
When this fountain is operating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:29.440
Apollo appears to emerge dramatically&nbsp;
from the water in his four-horse chariot.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.000
Beyond these fountains stretches the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
enormous Grand Canal extending the&nbsp;
central axis far into the horizon.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.200
The secondary axis is anchored to&nbsp;
the north by the basin of Neptune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:02.000
the largest fountain pool in the complex, and to&nbsp;
the south by the Pond of the Swiss, an artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.280
lake that also functions as a drainage basin,&nbsp;
collecting the water from the rest of the complex.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.880
The apparent symmetry of the&nbsp;
gardens is only superficial.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.800
Each of these square groves&nbsp;
of trees, known as bosquets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.080
contains its own unique fountain,&nbsp;
sculpture, or architectural feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:30.640
so that every space reveals a distinct experience&nbsp;
despite belonging to a rigorously ordered plan.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.920
At the same time Louis XIV commissioned&nbsp;
the architect Louis Le Vau to expand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.400
his father’s château in order to&nbsp;
accommodate the growing royal court.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:46.400
The king asked Le Vau to incorporate the existing&nbsp;
buildings as much as possible in the new design.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.920
Le Vau respected this wish by enveloping&nbsp;
the original building within a much larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:06:00.160
composition, adding two new wings linked&nbsp;
by a great terrace open towards the garden.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.560
The original brick and stone elevation&nbsp;
facing the forecourt was retained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.160
but the courtyard itself was&nbsp;
enlarged by demolishing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:14.880
low arcade that enclosed it, and adding&nbsp;
two wings terminating in temple facades.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.440
The courtyard was then paved in marble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:26.000
giving it the name by which it’s&nbsp;
still known today: Cour de Marbre.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.800
In 1678, architect Jules Hardouin-Mansart&nbsp;
enlarged the palace once again.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.440
He sacrificed the coherence and scale&nbsp;
of the original design by covering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:44.000
central terrace with a long gallery known as&nbsp;
the “Galerie des Glaces”, or Hall of Mirrors.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.840
This gallery features an extravagant decoration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.800
lining the walls with great mirrors which&nbsp;
created an extraordinary abundance of light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:00.720
and an apparent extension of the space&nbsp;
into eternity, typical of French Baroque.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.040
Mansart also added two great wings to the&nbsp;
north and south to accommodate the ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:17.600
increasing number of courtiers, bringing the total&nbsp;
length of the facade to almost half a kilometer.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.520
Because of the covering of the terrace&nbsp;
and the addition of the great wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.960
the facade becomes long and monotonous.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.360
But judging it as if it were a self-contained&nbsp;
Renaissance palace misses the point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:36.400
because the main theme of Mansart’s project is the&nbsp;
same to that of the gardens: infinite extension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:44.480
elongating the transverse axis that becomes&nbsp;
the edge between the town and the gardens.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.320
Space behaves very differently&nbsp;
on each side of the palace:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.160
on the urban side we see enclosed spaces and&nbsp;
squares, and elements that we would find in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:00.320
city, even the palace facade becoming an&nbsp;
enclosed space with the Court of Marble.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.440
On the garden side, however, the opposite happens.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.160
We find that the palace becomes a monumental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:12.800
object whose presence dominates&nbsp;
and organizes all of the axes.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:18.000
Its repetitive Italian façade,&nbsp;
articulated by a rusticated basement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.680
a giant Ionic order, and an attic topped&nbsp;
with a balustrade and a series of statues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:30.320
acts almost like a backdrop against which&nbsp;
the gardens unfold toward the horizon.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:33.920
Although strongly influenced&nbsp;
by Italian palace architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:40.960
the projecting pavilions with their freestanding&nbsp;
columns and sculptures remain unmistakably French.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.000
If you look at it from above, you can see that&nbsp;
the palace is a clear limit between these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:57.360
different conditions, yet they are not entirely&nbsp;
different: both of them unfold in radiating axes.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.720
On the city side you have these three wide&nbsp;
long avenues superimposed in an orthogonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:09.200
urban layout, replicating the trident that&nbsp;
originates in the Piazza del Popolo in Rome.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.680
In Versailles, however, the&nbsp;
symbolism becomes even more explicit.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.000
The avenues radiate from the center&nbsp;
of the palace like rays of sunlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:25.440
an unmistakable reference to Louis XIV,&nbsp;
who cultivated the image of the Sun King.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.920
The same idea continues in the gardens.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:33.520
The central axis seems to penetrate and&nbsp;
displace the central part of the palace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.080
extending uninterrupted through the gardens toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:41.520
the Grand Canal and continuing&nbsp;
indefinitely toward the horizon.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.280
Rather than ending at the palace, the axis makes&nbsp;
the building part of an infinite spatial sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.920
transforming architecture into a symbol&nbsp;
of the king's limitless authority.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.960
The smaller radiating patterns in the&nbsp;
gardens also echo these rays of light.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.840
As the axis extends farther from the&nbsp;
palace, the surrounding spaces become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:12.480
progressively larger and farther apart, allowing&nbsp;
the composition to remain legible from the palace.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:18.080
Between the palace and the town lies the&nbsp;
Place d'Armes, an immense forecourt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.400
prepares the ceremonial approach to Versailles.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.200
At its center stands an equestrian statue of Louis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:33.360
XIV modeled after the ancient statue of&nbsp;
Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius in Rome.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.960
Across the space, in the angles formed by the&nbsp;
three avenues, influenced by the twin churches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:48.400
in Rome’s Piazza del Popolo, Mansart designed two&nbsp;
horseshoe-shaped stables framing the three axes.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:51.920
Perhaps the most extraordinary&nbsp;
aspect of Versailles is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:57.040
none of these axes are centered on&nbsp;
a throne room or a ceremonial hall.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:00.560
Instead, everything revolves&nbsp;
around the king's bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:04.080
located in the exact center of&nbsp;
the palace, facing the marble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:10.080
courtyard—the King's Bedchamber became the&nbsp;
symbolic center of the entire composition.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.400
From this single room, the palace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:18.080
the gardens, and even the town appear&nbsp;
to unfold around the monarch himself.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.680
The room was richly decorated&nbsp;
with marble and gilded wood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.080
carvings as a display of wealth and authority.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.360
Chairs and seats were arranged for selected nobles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.320
who attended the kings waking and bed&nbsp;
time, which were public ceremonies.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:40.880
Being invited to witness the king sleeping was&nbsp;
a prestigious honor and a sign of royal favor.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.720
My favorite detail is this&nbsp;
round window facing southeast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.840
through which the rising sun enters every morning.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:53.760
To me this architectural gesture seems to&nbsp;
encapsulate the entire idea behind Versailles:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.840
not only the architecture, but even the movement&nbsp;
of the sun itself revolves around the king.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:07.520
Mansart designed a royal chapel in the northern&nbsp;
wing, accessible only from inside the palace.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.160
Although decorated with classical detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:16.960
its structure and proportions have a&nbsp;
clear influence from Gothic architecture.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:23.600
The chapel’s interior is articulated by an&nbsp;
arcaded basement and a colonnaded gallery above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.520
repeating the exterior articulation of the palace.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:30.720
The chapel consists of two stories:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:34.960
the ground floor with a smaller&nbsp;
scale, occupied by the courtiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:41.280
and the upper gallery reserved for the king,&nbsp;
directly connected to his private apartments.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:47.680
During Mass, the king literally occupied&nbsp;
a higher level than everyone else.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:51.760
One of the most impressive spaces at&nbsp;
Versailles is the vaulted gallery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:57.760
the Orangerie, a monumental hall designed&nbsp;
to shelter the royal collection of citrus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:04.800
trees during the winter while admitting&nbsp;
natural light through its huge arcade.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:10.400
Over 150 meters long, the south-facing&nbsp;
gallery is covered by a huge barrel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:14.800
vault designed by Mansart, whose&nbsp;
restrained yet monumental stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:21.040
architecture combines functional&nbsp;
efficiency with classical grandeur.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:26.000
At the end of the gallery stands Gianlorenzo&nbsp;
Bernini's equestrian statue of Louis XIV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:33.040
which reinforces the gallery's strong&nbsp;
central axis and ceremonial character.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.480
The gallery functions as a retaining structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:46.240
creating the terrace that separates the palace&nbsp;
above from the sunken Orangerie parterre below.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:57.040
In order to retreat from the court life in&nbsp;
the palace, Louis XIV commissioned Mansart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:02.720
the construction of a retreat home called the&nbsp;
Grand Trianon, connected to the palace via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:10.640
another grand axis. Like the rest of Mansart’s&nbsp;
buildings, his theme is the infinite extension.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:15.760
To achieve this he reduces the architecture&nbsp;
to its essential elements, basing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:21.840
articulation on a simple uniform system of&nbsp;
pilasters carrying a straight entablature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:28.320
and emphasized by arched French windows,&nbsp;
making the building almost an open skeleton.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.720
The building has an openwork&nbsp;
portico open to two sides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:44.160
allowing to appreciate the&nbsp;
flower beds planted by Le Notre.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.400
Versailles is perhaps the ultimate&nbsp;
expression of the Baroque spirit.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:54.560
Its spaces are revealed gradually and cannot be&nbsp;
experienced all at once, inviting you to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.560 --> 00:15:02.080
continuously through an architecture of unfolding&nbsp;
sequences and carefully orchestrated perspectives.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:08.880
This new conception of space had emerged about&nbsp;
a century earlier in Rome, as Baroque architects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:14.960
began to challenge the static harmony&nbsp;
and humanist ideals of the Renaissance.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:19.760
Renaissance architecture also valued&nbsp;
centralization, but its compositions remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:28.240
fundamentally enclosed and self-contained:&nbsp;
Italian gardens are finite, squares are enclosed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:36.880
palaces are compact blocks, statues and&nbsp;
buildings remain isolated in the landscape.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:44.160
At Versailles, that static conception&nbsp;
of space is replaced by one of movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:49.120
contrast, and seemingly infinite lines.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:53.120
Interior and exterior cease&nbsp;
to behave as separate worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:56.400
becoming part of a single continuous composition.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:01.280
What had begun as a modest hunting&nbsp;
lodge for Louis XIII became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:07.280
center of a complete ideal city which&nbsp;
seems to have an infinite extension.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:13.120
Louis XIV seems to be on one hand&nbsp;
trying to build Rome at Versailles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.920
replicating many elements in a larger&nbsp;
scale, but there’s also the notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:24.160
of reflection between city and gardens&nbsp;
which culminates not just in the palace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:28.640
but in the center of the palace with&nbsp;
the Hall of Mirrors, where you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:36.560
yourself with the gardens behind reflected,&nbsp;
creating that infinite illusion of space.

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:40.720
Versailles became the model for&nbsp;
royal residences throughout Europe.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:47.840
Its urban planning, gardens, ceremonial&nbsp;
spaces, and architecture were imitated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.840 --> 00:17:08.400
from Russia to Spain as monarchs sought to&nbsp;
project the same image of absolute authority.

